# ntlibc, compiled by the tcc the chain has just finished building.
#
# The work is done by ntlibc's own boot/kaem/build-i386.kaem, which its
# tools/gen-kaem.sh writes from what `make -n' would have run.  That script
# is checked in there and regenerated by a hook, so it cannot drift from the
# Makefile as sources come and go -- which is the whole reason to drive the
# build with it rather than to restate its 226 compiler invocations here.
#
# It names everything it needs in five variables and nothing on PATH, which
# is what lets it run at all on this side: kaem searches PATH only since
# stage0-pe32 ce81886d, and even now wine does not carry a unix PATH into
# the Windows environment for it to search.  Absolute paths, passed in, are
# the arrangement that does not depend on either.
#
#   srcdir      the source tree, read-only, wherever the store put it
#   CC          the compiler, and nothing else on the same variable: the
#               script's archiver line is `${CC} -ar rcs ...', and tcc reads
#               -ar only as its own first argument
#   bin_mkdir   \
#   bin_cp       > mescc-tools-extra, as everywhere else in this bootstrap
#   bin_catm    /
#
# Outputs land relative to the working directory, so this changes into
# ${NIX_BUILD_TOP} first and copies the two results out afterwards.
#
# NIX_BUILD_TOP and not ${TMPDIR}, though they name the same directory:
# wine drops TMPDIR on the way into the Windows environment, so it arrives
# empty and `cd' is left with no argument at all.

cd ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}

${bin_kaem} --verbose --strict --file ${srcdir}/boot/kaem/build-i386.kaem

${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/lib
${bin_cp} lib/libc.a ${out}/lib/libc.a
${bin_cp} lib/crt1.o ${out}/lib/crt1.o

# bits/alltypes.h is the one header the build generates rather than ships
# (catm of two per-arch halves), so it cannot be reached through the source
# tree the way the rest of include/ can.  Copied out here so a consumer has
# somewhere to point -I at for it.
${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/include/bits
${bin_cp} obj/include/bits/alltypes.h ${out}/include/bits/alltypes.h

# The import library for ntdll, which every ntlibc program links against:
# ntlibc reaches the kernel through named ntdll exports rather than through
# a syscall instruction, so the .def naming them is part of what it ships.
${bin_cp} ${srcdir}/tools/ntdll.def ${out}/lib/ntdll.def
